diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** None. All subsystems — Layer 2 (no duplicate MACs, no DOWN active interfaces), traffic control (no shaping qdiscs), OSPF/FRR control plane (all adjacencies Full, 18 routes on every router), and resource pressure — are healthy at the time of investigation.

**Mechanism (why the claim cannot be substantiated):** The helpdesk ticket describes Campus 1 (172.16.10/20/30) as unreachable from the rest of the network, with router16 suspected. A complete topology-wide reachability sweep shows 0% loss on all 28 tested paths including every cross-campus combination. OSPF is fully converged: all 9 routers carry 18 consistent routes, router16 has Full adjacency with both mswitch0 (via bond0/10.2.2.x) and router7 (via eth2/172.30.1.x), and the 172.16.x.x Campus 1 subnets propagate correctly through the domain.

**Localization:** Router16's carrier-change counters (2 on bond0, eth0, eth1, eth2) match the lab-wide startup-only baseline seen on router7 and router11 — no elevated counts that would indicate a real flap. FRR logs are empty. No flap script or stress process exists. All active interfaces show zero errors and zero drops.

**Root cause:** No fault is present or evidenced. The reported symptom is unconfirmed — there is no current reachability failure, no mechanism that could produce a recurring Campus 1 isolation, and no historical trace (log, counter, or process) consistent with the described event.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the event recurs, capture real-time carrier counters (`cat /sys/class/net/*/carrier_changes`) and OSPF adjacency state (`vtysh -c "show ip ospf neighbor"`) on router16 at the time of the outage to identify a transient link or routing fault.
=== END ===